From ce053eea1b4ceaa1f8fc706b52966795fb2df9cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Frdlik <michaelf.ms@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 19:46:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/14] Thylacine CL-7b-2: a process-symbols JITDylib for a
 host with no loader

LLJIT links process symbols by default, and the default setup reaches them the
only way a hosted platform can: EPCDynamicLibrarySearchGenerator ->
SelfExecutorProcessControl::loadDylib(nullptr) -> dlopen(NULL). Every Thylacine
binary is statically linked and musl's static dlopen is a stub, so
LLJITBuilder::create() itself returned "Dynamic loading not supported" and
llvmpipe never came up.

Two simpler repairs are both wrong, which is why this file exists rather than a
one-liner.

Making dlopen(NULL) succeed with a sentinel handle buys an empty namespace:
SelfExecutorProcessControl::lookupSymbolsAsync resolves through
DynamicLibrary::getAddressOfSymbol(), which calls HandleSet::DLSym() on that
handle directly and never consults SearchForAddressOfSymbol() -- so
DynamicLibrary::AddSymbol(), the mechanism that exists for exactly this
situation, is not on the path.

setLinkProcessSymbolsByDefault(false) fails one step later and just as
fatally: setUpGenericLLVMIRPlatform() opens by requiring a process-symbols
JITDylib and errors with "Native platforms require a process symbols JITDylib".

setProcessSymbolsJITDylibSetup() is the seam that fits. The platform gets its
JITDylib, the default link order is unchanged, and the contents come from a
table fixed at link time instead of a loader at run time -- the honest
description of a static system.

Each entry takes the address of the real function, so a name this sysroot does
not provide is a build error rather than a silent hole. The signature in each
macro is load-bearing, not decoration: <cmath> injects C++ overloads of the
double-named functions into the global namespace, so a bare &::acos does not
compile at all. That was caught by compiling the table, not by reading it.

The table is deliberately "what a static host can name", not "what llvmpipe
needs" -- the second set is not knowable from here. A symbol the JIT wants and
this table lacks fails at lookup, by name, in ORC's usual "Symbols not found",
which is the diagnostic that says what to add.
---
 .../llvm/ExecutionEngine/Orc/ExecutionUtils.h |  14 ++
 llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/Orc/CMakeLists.txt   |   1 +
 .../Orc/ThylacineProcessSymbols.cpp           | 156 ++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 171 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/Orc/ThylacineProcessSymbols.cpp

diff --git a/llvm/include/llvm/ExecutionEngine/Orc/ExecutionUtils.h b/llvm/include/llvm/ExecutionEngine/Orc/ExecutionUtils.h
index e42c90256..bb6944346 100644
--- a/llvm/include/llvm/ExecutionEngine/Orc/ExecutionUtils.h
+++ b/llvm/include/llvm/ExecutionEngine/Orc/ExecutionUtils.h
@@ -383,6 +383,20 @@ private:
   ObjectLinkingLayer &L;
 };
 
+#if defined(__thylacine__)
+
+class LLJIT;
+
+/// Build the process-symbols JITDylib for a statically linked Thylacine host.
+///
+/// Pass to LLJITBuilder::setProcessSymbolsJITDylibSetup(). The default setup
+/// reaches process symbols via dlopen(NULL), which a static musl binary cannot
+/// do; this one populates the JITDylib from a table fixed at link time. See
+/// ThylacineProcessSymbols.cpp for why the two simpler repairs do not work.
+LLVM_ABI Expected<JITDylibSP> thylacineProcessSymbolsSetup(LLJIT &J);
+
+#endif // __thylacine__
+
 } // end namespace orc
 } // end namespace llvm
 
